00:00:02,050 --> 00:00:04,822
So here's the merge sort algorithm we worked

2
00:00:04,822 --> 00:00:04,998
on.

3
00:00:05,559 --> 00:00:07,177
Now in this algorithm,

4
00:00:07,802 --> 00:00:11,054
we are doing a bunch of things, and up to

5
00:00:11,070 --> 00:00:14,050
this point we have no impact on space

6
00:00:14,050 --> 00:00:14,659
complexity.

7
00:00:15,315 --> 00:00:17,254
But then here we do something which is a

8
00:00:17,254 --> 00:00:20,778
great trigger for us. We call ourselves, we

9
00:00:20,778 --> 00:00:22,028
call ourselves

10
00:00:22,925 --> 00:00:25,985
with shorter arrays, but together they give

11
00:00:25,985 --> 00:00:28,773
us one long array, basically our original

12
00:00:28,773 --> 00:00:30,631
array, and we do this recursively.

13
00:00:31,418 --> 00:00:33,570
And therefore we create a bunch of new

14
00:00:33,682 --> 00:00:34,453
arrays in memory,

15
00:00:35,031 --> 00:00:37,343
and the number of arrays and the values

16
00:00:37,552 --> 00:00:39,961
stored in these arrays, so the size in

17
00:00:40,009 --> 00:00:42,964
memory occupied by those values in those

18
00:00:43,012 --> 00:00:43,285
arrays,

19
00:00:43,879 --> 00:00:47,798
that simply depends on n. The longer the

20
00:00:47,814 --> 00:00:50,769
array we pass in here, the more we'll split

21
00:00:50,849 --> 00:00:53,402
up our arrays here, and the more new arrays

22
00:00:53,434 --> 00:00:55,859
with new values are getting created. And we

23
00:00:55,859 --> 00:00:57,722
have a linear relation here

24
00:00:58,557 --> 00:01:00,243
The while loop doesn't really make a

25
00:01:00,243 --> 00:01:02,716
difference there. We don't call ourselves

26
00:01:02,829 --> 00:01:04,740
recursively or anything like that, and we're

27
00:01:04,900 --> 00:01:06,699
only working with temporary values.

28
00:01:07,229 --> 00:01:09,589
But because of that recursion, we have a

29
00:01:09,589 --> 00:01:13,845
space complexity of O here for our merge

30
00:01:14,086 --> 00:01:15,531
sort algorithm.

31
00:01:16,559 --> 00:01:18,888
And that completes our table here.

32
00:01:19,626 --> 00:01:22,661
So overall, space complexity actually isn't

33
00:01:22,661 --> 00:01:25,584
too tricky, and very, very, very often you

34
00:01:25,616 --> 00:01:28,821
will have either constant or linear space

35
00:01:28,821 --> 00:01:31,963
complexity. You typically have linear space

36
00:01:31,963 --> 00:01:34,014
complexity if you call yourselves

37
00:01:34,110 --> 00:01:34,735
recursively.

38
00:01:35,392 --> 00:01:37,316
And of course, theoretically, you can have

39
00:01:37,396 --> 00:01:39,832
other space complexities like quadratic

40
00:01:39,832 --> 00:01:42,092
space complexity and so on as well.

41
00:01:42,717 --> 00:01:45,746
Maybe if you have recursive calls inside of

42
00:01:45,746 --> 00:01:46,147
a loop,

43
00:01:46,868 --> 00:01:49,737
but that is not that often the case.

44
00:01:50,378 --> 00:01:52,125
And as I mentioned in the beginning of this

45
00:01:52,237 --> 00:01:54,705
module, space complexity in general

46
00:01:55,267 --> 00:01:57,693
is typically not the most important

47
00:01:57,693 --> 00:02:00,216
dimension which you can use to compare

48
00:02:00,409 --> 00:02:03,044
algorithms, but instead it's just an

49
00:02:03,140 --> 00:02:05,326
additional dimension which you should be

50
00:02:05,326 --> 00:02:08,186
aware of and about which you now learned how

51
00:02:08,202 --> 00:02:10,532
